we have some breaking news we can bring  you now this is about a legal challenge  the government's plans to deport some  migrants to Rwanda while the high court  has upheld the home office plans this  has been rumbling on in the courts for  quite a while the government has argued  that it should be able to deport people  that it chooses to to Rwanda while their  applications are assessed a number of  different legal challenges to this  decision have happened over the last few  months which has delayed the plan it's  not a single person has been as yet  deported to Rwanda under this scheme  we've got some words from the court Lord  Justice Lewis says it is lawful for  government to make arrangements to  relocate for Asylum claims to be  determined in Rwanda rather than the  United Kingdom he goes on to say it's  consistent with Refugee convention  however he also said the Home Secretary  must consider properly the circumstances  of each individual claimants particular  circumstances and also said that the  Home Secretary has not probably  considered the circumstances of eight  individual claimants so the cases have  been set aside and referred back to the  Home Secretary so it is complex but  essentially the government have been  told that they can Deport people to  Rwanda but the eight people that have  brought this legal challenge are not  automatically going to be deported to  Rwanda because in the judge's view their  cases haven't been considered  individually in enough detail so the  government will have to now reconsider  all of their cases in Greater detail but  the judge appears to have said that in  Broad principle the principle of the UK  government deporting people to Rwanda  while their Asylum claims are determined  is lawful
 

